WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
This is a neural network learning to walk.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.591
Nobody trained it with gradients.

00:00:05.591 --> 00:00:10.498
It was evolved, weights and shape together, by an algorithm called NEAT.

00:00:11.098 --> 00:00:13.005
This is Bipedal Walker.

00:00:13.005 --> 00:00:17.804
A box-shaped body sits on two legs, and each leg has a hip and a knee.

00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:21.795
The ground is bumpy, and it is different every time.

00:00:22.395 --> 00:00:30.741
At every step, the walker sets four motor torques, one for each joint, each somewhere between minus one and plus one.

00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:37.439
The goal is to walk to the far end without falling, within one thousand six hundred steps.

00:00:37.439 --> 00:00:41.411
That is about thirty two seconds of simulated time.

00:00:42.011 --> 00:00:45.643
To decide, the walker senses twenty four numbers.

00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:51.741
Four describe the body: how it is tilted, how fast it spins, and how fast it moves.

00:00:52.341 --> 00:00:59.563
Ten describe the legs: the angle and speed of every joint, and whether each foot is touching the ground.

00:01:00.163 --> 00:01:06.042
And ten come from a lidar, a fan of rays that measures how far away the ground is ahead.

00:01:06.042 --> 00:01:11.670
All of them are scaled to a fixed range, so a trained walker is one small file.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.243
The score comes from the environment itself.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:20.459
Moving forward earns points, about three hundred for walking the whole course.

00:01:21.059 --> 00:01:26.427
Every bit of motor effort costs a little, so flailing lowers the score.

00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:29.040
And falling is expensive.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.919
If the body touches the ground, the episode ends with minus one hundred.

00:01:33.919 --> 00:01:40.258
The task counts as solved at an average of three hundred, over one hundred random terrains.

00:01:40.858 --> 00:01:43.449
The pilot is a small neural network.

00:01:43.449 --> 00:01:50.640
The twenty four sensor values come in on the left and flow through hidden nodes to four outputs on the right.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.155
Here it is while walking.

00:01:53.155 --> 00:01:56.637
Blue means a positive signal, orange a negative one.

00:01:56.637 --> 00:01:59.664
Thicker lines are stronger connections.

00:02:00.264 --> 00:02:06.050
The four outputs on the right are the joint torques, sent to the motors at every step.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:11.904
This project had one hard rule: the network must have at least one hidden layer.

00:02:11.904 --> 00:02:20.743
So every network starts as twenty four inputs, eight hidden nodes and four outputs, with no shortcut from an input straight to a motor.

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:25.068
Evolution could still remove nodes, so the code checks.

00:02:25.068 --> 00:02:29.266
It counts the hidden nodes on a real path from input to output.

00:02:29.266 --> 00:02:34.887
A network with none gets its fitness cut, and it can never be saved as a champion.

00:02:35.487 --> 00:02:39.864
NEAT starts with a population of two hundred random networks.

00:02:39.864 --> 00:02:46.001
Each one is described by a genome: a list of its nodes and its weighted connections.

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:49.640
Children differ from their parents by mutation.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.071
Most mutations just change a weight.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.950
Some add a new connection.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.928
And some add a whole new node, by splitting a connection in two.

00:02:57.928 --> 00:03:00.328
That is how the networks grow.

00:03:00.928 --> 00:03:05.068
Each generation, the best fifth survive and have children.

00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:09.270
A child is a copy, or a mix of two parents, plus mutations.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.330
The two very best pass on unchanged.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:18.773
Similar networks are grouped into species, and they mostly compete within their own species.

00:03:18.773 --> 00:03:23.104
That gives a new structure a few generations to prove itself.

00:03:23.704 --> 00:03:25.472
How good is a network?

00:03:25.472 --> 00:03:31.188
Every network walks the same three random terrains, and its fitness is the average score.

00:03:31.188 --> 00:03:35.549
The terrains change every generation, so nobody can memorise them.

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:38.972
Later in the run, that went up to 5 terrains.

00:03:39.572 --> 00:03:46.329
To save time, an episode stops early if the walker makes no real progress for one hundred and fifty steps.

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:52.437
A network that just stands there does not waste one thousand six hundred steps of compute.

00:03:53.037 --> 00:03:54.895
But this creates a trap.

00:03:54.895 --> 00:03:57.623
Standing still scores about minus nine.

00:03:57.623 --> 00:04:01.106
A brave attempt that falls scores minus one hundred.

00:04:01.106 --> 00:04:03.786
So at first, standing still wins.

00:04:04.386 --> 00:04:07.602
The best score in each generation is noisy.

00:04:07.602 --> 00:04:10.929
Three terrains can be lucky, or unlucky.

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:18.560
So every ten generations, the top five networks walk ten terrains they have never seen, with no early stop.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.372
The best average becomes the champion.

00:04:21.372 --> 00:04:23.506
That is the blue line.

00:04:24.106 --> 00:04:25.927
Here is the whole run.

00:04:25.927 --> 00:04:30.843
For the first 70 generations or so, the best networks simply stood still.

00:04:30.843 --> 00:04:32.478
The trap held.

00:04:33.078 --> 00:04:39.811
Then, around generation 69, a network found a slow shuffle that moved forward without falling.

00:04:39.811 --> 00:04:44.402
Its score jumped above zero, and the population followed.

00:04:45.002 --> 00:04:48.748
The champion passed 100 at generation 99.

00:04:48.748 --> 00:04:59.222
The scores had levelled off, so from generation 430 each network got 5 terrains instead of three, and the population grew to 300.

00:04:59.222 --> 00:05:05.103
After 674 generations, its average on ten unseen terrains is 200.

00:05:05.703 --> 00:05:12.541
Here are the champions side by side, each on a typical terrain: the middle one of its ten test terrains.

00:05:12.541 --> 00:05:16.504
From a random network, to a shuffle, to a real walk.

00:05:17.104 --> 00:05:20.434
And this is the final champion.

00:05:25.104 --> 00:05:28.060
While the scores rose, the networks grew.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:34.756
The violet line counts hidden nodes in use, and the amber line counts layers from input to output.

00:05:35.356 --> 00:05:41.031
The champion has 6 hidden nodes in 5 hidden layers, and only 37 connections.

00:05:41.031 --> 00:05:46.166
It started with eight hidden nodes in one layer and 224 connections.

00:05:46.166 --> 00:05:52.479
Evolution threw most of them away, and stacked what was left into a deeper chain.

00:05:53.079 --> 00:05:55.323
This is the number of species.

00:05:55.323 --> 00:06:05.604
A setting called the compatibility threshold decides how different two networks must be to count as separate species, and the code tunes it to aim for about ten.

00:06:06.204 --> 00:06:15.829
In the first hour, that tuning ran away. neat python never merges species, so the count barely fell, and the threshold kept climbing, to thirty eight.

00:06:15.829 --> 00:06:21.002
It is now kept within limits, and the run continued from its checkpoint.

00:06:21.602 --> 00:06:27.655
The real test is one hundred terrains the champion has never seen.

00:06:27.655 --> 00:06:29.864
Its average is 179.

00:06:29.864 --> 00:06:34.336
Half of its runs score between 191 and 220.

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:39.586
But it falls in 17 of the one hundred, and each fall costs a lot.

00:06:39.586 --> 00:06:45.110
That is what pulls the average down, and it is the main thing left to fix.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:56.211
For comparison, gradient based methods on the same laptop reached about two hundred and seventy with PPO, and just over three hundred with TD3.

00:06:56.211 --> 00:07:01.657
NEAT is 93 points behind PPO, without computing a single gradient.

00:07:02.257 --> 00:07:08.200
All of this ran on a four core laptop with no graphics card, in 8 hours of training.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:14.443
Every network is evaluated on its own, so evolution spreads easily across the cores.

00:07:15.043 --> 00:07:16.349
No gradients.

00:07:16.349 --> 00:07:17.282
No GPU.

00:07:17.282 --> 00:07:21.761
Just a population of networks, mutating, competing, and growing.

00:07:21.761 --> 00:07:26.105
The code, the clips and the data are on the project page.
